 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to salary adjustments according to plan and step-ups or increments",
    "basePrintNo": "S6788",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6788-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3669, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6788\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public authorities law, in\nrelation to salary adjustments according to plan and step-ups or\nincrements\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF THE BILL :\n\nTo prevent certain suspensions of step up increases for the employees\nof the county in the event of a wage freeze declaration by the\nauthority.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 amends section 3669 Of the public authorities law\n\nSection 2 sets an effective immediate date, which will be considered\nin full force and effect on October 1st, 2016.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis bill would provide a certain guarantee that employees will\nreceive the contractual step ups that they should realize when they\nwork towards the actual base pay that they were hired for as long as\nthe County's four year multi year plan is accepted by the Nassau\nInterim Finance Authority. The bill provides that if the four-year\nplan has been accepted by NIFA, any wage freeze resolutions by NIFA\nshall not include steps/increments during that four-year period.\nStep-ups are unique in labor contracts in that they are not a cost of\nliving wage increase, but rather a series of steps/years to reach top\npay. The top step base salary of an employee reflects the salary\nagreed upon by the employer and the union to be paid to an employee in\na specific title commensurate with the standard work to be performed\nby that title. This will help with the recruitment and retention\nissues that can plague employers in hiring. When the Nassau County\nInterim finance Authority implemented a wage freeze on the employees\nof the county of Nassau, Labor contributed over $270 million dollars\nin savings. Furthermore, the unions entered into memorandums of\nagreements (wage freeze concession deals) amending all of their\nrespective contracts which caused a permanent decrease in labor costs\ninto the future. During the litigation of the NIFA wage freeze the\nfederal courts have held the that The Second circuit case of the\nBuffalo wage freeze: Rachel's Fed, n v. Tobe 464 F.3d 362 (2D Cir.\n2206( (Buffalo) points out that wage freezes should be done as a final\nlast resort measure, not to be used as a collective bargaining tool.\nThis bill would be consistent with that belief.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNone\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATION :\n\nNone\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately and to be considered in full force and effect on October\n1st, 2016\n",
